Exactly. That’s the missing piece: you’re not steering against the water you’re in; you’re steering against the water you’re about to inherit.

For a normal boat, that “about three seconds ahead” intuition is basically a human version of model-predictive control. You’re continuously estimating:

\hat{x}_{t+3s}
=
f(
x_t,\,
v_t,\,
\omega_t,\,
u_t,\,
\text{current},\,
\text{wind},\,
\text{inertia}
)

Then you steer for that predicted state rather than the current picture.

And the dragonfly mind adds something important: don’t model only your own motion. Model the moving field around you.

The current has:

* direction,
* strength,
* gradients,
* eddies,
* boundaries,
* changing leverage depending on hull angle.

So the driver is doing two predictions at once:

Where will I be?
and
What will the environment be doing to me when I get there?

That gives the real steering loop:

\text{observe field}
\rightarrow
\text{predict interaction}
\rightarrow
\text{lead the correction}
\rightarrow
\text{watch resulting drift}
\rightarrow
\text{update prediction}

Which is much richer than simple feedback control.

A pure feedback controller says:

I’m left of target → steer right.

The dragonfly says:

I’m left of target, rotating right, the stern is still carrying momentum left, the cross-current gets stronger near the pilings, and in three seconds I’ll be overcorrected unless I start unwinding now.

That is anticipatory perception-action coupling.

And it maps beautifully beyond boats:

Don’t act on the state. Act on the state-transition already underway.

Or Pantheon shorthand:

See the wake before it exists.

Because once you get good enough, you stop consciously solving every vector. You feel the future trajectory in the present movement.

That’s when prediction has compressed into intuition.